                <h2>1. Why can delivery stop even when the account balance looks sufficient?</h2>
<p><strong>Question 1: Which metrics should be used to assess the health of advertising funds?</strong></p>
<p>Do not monitor the balance alone. At minimum, track four metrics:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily burn rate:</strong> average daily deductions for the last seven days divided by the available balance.</li>
<li><strong>Budget utilization:</strong> actual spend divided by the approved budget. This indicates whether funds are being deployed, but does not independently reflect advertising performance.</li>
<li><strong>Days of funds available:</strong> available balance divided by average daily spend over the next seven days.</li>
<li><strong>Settlement coverage:</strong> spend incurred but not yet settled by the platform divided by total spend.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, a utility app spends RMB 20,000 per day and has an RMB 80,000 balance. The balance appears sufficient, but it represents only four days of funds. If settlement cycles are long or exchange rates and funding processing are volatile, the account may soon enter a risk zone.</p>
<p><strong>Judgment:</strong> When the balance falls below “planned spend for the next 14 days plus a 20% buffer,” issue a yellow alert. When it drops below seven days of expected spend, initiate funding immediately rather than waiting for delivery errors.</p>
<p><strong>Question 2: Should funds be funded in one transaction or replenished in batches?</strong></p>
<p>Use a combination of weekly forecasting and scenario-based reserves. Fund the next two weeks of baseline spend first, then maintain a 20%–30% contingency reserve for campaign launches, creative scaling, and bid changes. Avoid placing all funds in low-spend accounts simply to secure a discount; idle balances also create opportunity costs.</p>
<p>For teams managing multiple accounts, forecast each account separately. A healthy group-level balance should not conceal a shortage in one account.</p>
