<h2>2. When the ledger does not match, trace the funding chain</h2>
<p><strong>Question 3: How should discrepancies between finance records and platform spend be investigated?</strong></p>
<p>Align the definitions before judging the difference. Platform spend generally reflects advertising costs incurred, while actual finance deductions may also include account-level charges, taxes, platform fees, exchange rates, and settlement delays. The two figures should not be compared mechanically.</p>
<p>Use the following sequence:</p>
<ol>
<li>Reconcile currencies, exchange-rate dates, and tax-inclusive or tax-exclusive definitions.</li>
<li>Match transaction details by account, date, and ad set.</li>
<li>Separate spend that has not been deducted from deductions that have not yet appeared as spend.</li>
<li>Check refunds, chargebacks, promotional credits, and balance adjustments.</li>
<li>Freeze automatic funding for accounts with unexplained discrepancies on two consecutive days and conduct a manual review.</li>
</ol>
<p>If settlement timing explains the difference, establish an expected posting date. If a deduction cannot be explained, suspend additional funding immediately and preserve platform records and payment documentation.</p>
<p><strong>Question 4: Should spend reconciliation be performed daily or monthly?</strong></p>
<p>The answer is daily monitoring with a monthly close. Review deductions, spend, and balance movements daily, paying particular attention to sudden spending spikes and repeated deduction anomalies. Produce a monthly report by account covering budget, spend, settlements, funding, refunds, and FX gains or losses, followed by finance approval. The reconciliation file should include account ID, currency, platform spend, actual deduction, discrepancy reason, and owner so that accounts are not commingled even when the total balance matches.</p>
<h2>3. Multi-account management requires hard rules</h2>
<p><strong>Question 5: How can a sudden account overspend be prevented while controlling total funding risk?</strong></p>
<p>Enterprise fund management should not depend entirely on manual monitoring. Establish four control levels:</p>
<ul>
<li>Below 14 days of spend: notify the account owner.</li>
<li>Below 10 days of spend: require additional approval for large funding transactions.</li>
<li>Below seven days of spend: suspend low-priority automated funding plans.</li>
<li>After an unexplained deduction, repeated chargeback, or account restriction: suspend new funding and escalate the case to risk review.</li>
</ul>
<p>Budget limits, funding approvals, balance alerts, and platform access should be managed as separate controls. Every funding, withdrawal, or payment-method change should retain an application, approval, and activity log.</p>
<p><strong>Final judgment:</strong> The objective of fund management is not to keep every account funded at all times. It is to prevent delivery interruptions while minimizing idle capital, unauthorized deductions, and reconciliation errors. Finance should own definitions and funding security, media buyers should control burn rates, and risk teams should govern permissions and suspension mechanisms. When all three teams work from the same daily data, fund management becomes an operational control system rather than a clerical task.</p>
